<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Zhao of Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 516–489 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Zhao of Chu ruled the State of Chu from 516 to 489 BCE. In 506 BCE the State of Wu, guided by the Chu exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a>, defeated Chu at Boju and took its capital Ying, and the king fled to the small state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>. He returned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> sent an army to help, restored Chu's power and died on campaign in 489 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xiong Zhen was the son of King Ping of Chu and his mother was Bo Ying. He had a sister and at least three elder half-brothers born of concubines, Shen, Jie and Qi, known also by the courtesy names Zixi, Ziqi and Zilü. He married Zhen Jiang, a daughter of the Marquess of Qi, and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-hui-of-chu">King Hui of Chu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The invasion of 506 BCE was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">King Helü of Wu</a>, with an army commanded by the strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a>, author of The Art of War, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a>, whose father and brother King Ping had killed. After the rout at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju">Battle of Boju</a> the Chu commander Nang Wa fled to the state of Zheng. The Chu general Shen Yin Shu defeated the Wu army but was badly wounded, and at his own request a Chu officer killed him.</p><p>In his flight King Zhao was wounded by a Chu arrow at Yunmeng, then went through Yun to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>. His minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-baoxu">Shen Baoxu</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> to ask for help. Duke Ai of Qin was at first non-committal, but after Shen had spent seven days kneeling and wailing in the palace courtyard he agreed to send troops, the Wu army withdrew, and the king returned to Ying.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> tells of a sheep-butcher named Yue who followed the king into flight and back. Offered rewards, and finally a place among the three highest nobles, he refused them all, saying he had fled from fear and not loyalty, and asked only to return to his stall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 491 BCE the king saw the omen known as clouds like numerous red birds pressing the day to fly. Not long afterwards King Fuchai of Wu, Helü's successor, attacked the State of Chen, which asked Chu for help. King Zhao led his troops in person and was killed in a battle that followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Zhao of Chu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It later became a close Chu ally and sheltered <b>King Zhao of Chu</b> when Wu armies took Ying in 506 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-sui">State of Sui</a></li></ul></section>
